A comprehensive global fit for the tau lepton electric dipole moment (EDM), incorporating existing constraints from both direct and indirect measurements, is conducted. Based on the analysis of previous results, this study specifically updates the indirect bounds derived from the rare decay $B_s^0 \to \tau^\pm l^\mp K^{\pm0}$. Utilizing updated experimental data from LHCb regarding branching fractions and charge asymmetry limits, along refined theoretical inputs from lattice QCD and perturbative QCD, significantly tightens these indirect limits. Consequently, the resulting 95% confidence level interval for the EDM, $d_\tau$, is established as
$$
\sqrt{}
|d_\tau| < 8.1 \times 10^{-17} \text{ eV}^{-1}
$$
with the indirect bound accounting for $75\%$ of the overall limit. Looking ahead, a dedicated search for charge asymmetry in the $B_s^0 \to \tau^+ \tau^- K^{\pm0}$ decay at LHCb, achieving a sensitivity of approximately $10^{-3}$, would effectively probe an EDM value near $2 \times 10^{-18} \text{ eV}^{-1}$ for the tau lepton.
